  "query_preview": "..Today through Monday...Strong high pressure \ncentered off the coast of Baja California will help to maintain \nfair weather conditions across northern Arizona today. Overall \nexpect dry conditions, light winds, and daytime temperatures 5-15 \ndegrees above normal. By tomorrow, the ridge to our west starts to\nretreat as a series of shortwaves begins brush past northern \nArizona. The first shortwave will brush to our north on Saturday, \nwith brief ridging on Sunday, then another shortwave on Monday. \nThese disturbances will remain mostly dry, with the primary \nimpacts being periods of breezier winds and cloudiness. Breezy \nconditions are forecast for Saturday and Monday where gusts to \n25-35 mph are possible.\n\nTuesday and onward...A stronger disturbance is forecast move across \nnorthern Arizona towards the middle of next week, however there are \nstill deviations among ensemble guidance on the intensity/trajectory \nof this feature. As of right now, the trough looks to dive southward \nover the east Pacific on Monday-Tuesday. Precipitation chances ramp \nup by Tuesday evening as moisture streams northward ahead of the \napproaching system, then continue through Wednesday evening as the \nlow passes overhead. There is low confidence in amounts, but the \ncurrent forecast brings widespread rainfall accumulations in the \nrange of 0.5-1.5 inches south of the Mogollon Rim, with lighter \naccumulations 0.2-0.5 north of the Rim. There is even less \nconfidence on the snow level, but we may see snow levels starting \naround 8000 ft at the onset and dropping down to 7000 ft towards the \nend. There is plenty of room for the details to change, so continue \nto check back for future updates on this period.",
